  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}+14t+8 & =0 \\\\ (3t+2)(t+4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(-4) & =2(-4)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{15}{2}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{\u2032}(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =2(-\\frac{2}{3})+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{6}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-4$ and $t=-\\frac{2}{3}.$",
